# Cursor AI Rules for Mira's Labyrinth Game

## Project Context
This is a 2D arcade labyrinth game built for a 7-year-old child. The game features a retro cartoon style with Blade Runner aesthetics (dark background, neon colors). The hero navigates through a maze using keyboard arrow keys.

## Tech Stack
- React 18 with Vite
- React-Konva for 2D canvas rendering
- Vanilla CSS for styling (no CSS-in-JS)
- JavaScript (can migrate to TypeScript if needed)

## Code Style & Conventions

### General
- Write clean, readable code with clear comments
- Keep functions small and focused
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Prefer functional components and hooks over class components

### React Components
- Use functional components with hooks
- Keep components in separate files
- Props should be documented with comments
- Use PropTypes or TypeScript for type checking
- Component file structure:
  ```jsx
  import statements
  
  // Component logic
  function ComponentName({ props }) {
    // hooks
    // handlers
    // render
  }
  
  export default ComponentName;
  ```

### File Organization
- Components go in `src/components/`
- Game logic goes in `src/game-logic/`
- Styles go in `src/styles/`
- Assets go in `public/assets/`
- Each major feature should have its own file

### Naming Conventions
- Components: PascalCase (e.g., `WelcomeScreen.jsx`)
- Utility functions: camelCase (e.g., `generateLabyrinth.js`)
- CSS classes: kebab-case (e.g., `.game-board`)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `MAX_LEVEL`)

### State Management
- Use React hooks (useState, useEffect, useContext)
- Keep game state in a central location or context
- Avoid prop drilling - use Context for global state
- Local component state for UI-only concerns

### Game Logic
- Separate game logic from UI components
- Labyrinth generation should be deterministic or seedable
- Keep collision detection efficient
- Game state transitions should be clear and explicit

### Styling
- Use the Blade Runner color scheme consistently:
  - Background: `#0a0a0a`
  - Neon Cyan: `#00f0ff`
  - Neon Magenta: `#ff00ff`
  - Neon Orange: `#ff6600`
  - Walls: `#1a1a2e`
  - Paths: `#16213e`
- Mobile-responsive design
- Use CSS custom properties for theme colors
- Keep styles modular and component-specific when needed

### Performance
- Optimize canvas rendering (use React-Konva efficiently)
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders
- Use useMemo and useCallback where appropriate
- Keep game loop efficient (60fps target)

### Kid-Friendly Features
- Large, easy-to-click buttons
- Clear visual feedback
- Simple, intuitive controls
- Encouraging messages
- No frustrating mechanics
- Gradual difficulty progression

### Code Comments
- Comment complex game logic
- Explain maze generation algorithms
- Document collision detection logic
- Add TODO comments for future features

### Error Handling
- Graceful error handling
- No crashes - game should always be playable
- Console errors should be informative for debugging

### Git Commits
- Use clear, descriptive commit messages
- Commit logical chunks of work
- Format: `feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `style:`, `docs:`

## Current Development Phase
- Phase 1: Basic setup and core mechanics
  - Welcome screen with name input
  - Simple labyrinth rendering
  - Hero as CSS square
  - Arrow key controls
  - Timer and win condition
  - Win modal

## Future Considerations
- Phase 2: Enhanced graphics (sprite images, animations)
- Phase 3: Multiple levels and progression
- Phase 4: Sound effects and music
- Phase 5: Additional features (power-ups, obstacles)

## Testing
- Test keyboard controls thoroughly
- Ensure win condition always triggers correctly
- Verify timer accuracy
- Test on different screen sizes
- Kid-test with target audience (7-year-old)

## AI Assistant Guidelines
- When suggesting code, prefer simple, readable solutions
- Consider the young target audience in all decisions
- Maintain the Blade Runner aesthetic in visual changes
- Keep game mechanics simple and fun
- Suggest child-appropriate enhancements
- Prioritize stability and playability over complex features

